{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Plan review BLOCKED with three findings. I verified all three against the tree; all three are right.\n\nFindings 2 and 3 are the same defect: the plan cited the verdict master as schemas/verify_signature_result.schema.json. That path does not exist — ls confirms only src/rebar/schemas/verify_signature_result.schema.json. As the reviewer notes, a census reading the cited path would error file-not-found instead of pinning anything. Corrected, and the plan now says the test reads it through rebar.schemas.load(\"verify_signature_result\") rather than by literal path, so the arm cannot break again on a path move.\n\nFinding 1 is the more useful one. AC4/AC5 said \"four registered sites\" and \"four mutation checks\", which matches neither reading of the drift section: five distinct enumerations, or three if counted per document. I had written \"four\" carelessly and it would have left an implementer guessing how many arms to write.\n\nCounting them properly surfaced something the plan had not noticed: the five arms do not share a parse shape, so the census must register ARMS, not files.\n1. reuse-surface.md verdicts — a markdown table with a backticked first column.\n2. oss-comparison types — one backtick span, pipe-joined with no spaces (task|story|bug|epic).\n3. oss-comparison statuses — same pipe-joined shape.\n4. oss-comparison relations — NOT a list at all, but the English word \"six\" in prose. It has to be checked as a COUNT against len(get_args(Relation)) and corrected to \"seven\"; registering it as a list arm would be wrong.\n5. user-guide statuses — one span, pipe-joined WITH spaces (idea | open | ...).\n\nThe plan now carries that arm table explicitly, AC4 requires five mutation checks (one per arm, with the count arm asserting expected-vs-actual count), and AC5 adds the could-not-locate requirement so a reworded passage blames the parser rather than the values — the same failure mode I hit implementing F14.\n\nfile_impact was already recorded and is unchanged, so the re-review runs on the same basis. Re-review started."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788403876140258001,"uuid":"2941ef24-2a80-4eff-a889-99054d1bfb2c"}